INITIAL CHECKPOINT / setup by 00a at 2026-09-06T23:25:43.102314+00:00

This is setup and evidence capture, not the first remediation check-in. No agent was killed, restarted, retried, or declared stalled/recovered during setup. Next action: the first sleep-60 monitor; the waking successor performs all four steps and then uses hourly monitors as needed.

Board sase-xs was created successfully as task(memory), size large, explicit codex/gpt-6-astra, assigned to supervisor root 00a, with sentinel path identifying this as a message-board experiment. It stays open to avoid unrelated task triage. No tracked repository source or actual memory files were edited.

Deduplication completed: searched memory tasks then all task types for message-board/supervision/continuous-monitor/agent-watch terms; swept the last week of memory tasks then all 52 task beads; reviewed all 27 in-progress epic summaries/children/descriptions. No semantic duplicate of this user-requested operational board or causally owning epic. Existing implementation epics are adjacent systems, not ownership of this experiment.

Commands verified: sase agent restart NAME --dry-run previews destructive scope; restart -m accepts provider/model; ordinary retries use allocate_retry_name and a prompt rewriter preserving metadata. src/sase/agent/relaunch_prompt.py additionally handles clan declarations; src/sase/agent/retry_prompt.py rewrites top-level identity while dropping obsolete family attachment. Use the appropriate helper for the real prompt. Restart preserves chat but deletes old run artifacts; save diagnosis first. sase monitor start accepts the command after --, not --command. Omit --model to preserve the supervisor model/effort, and use a timeout greater than the intentional sleep.

Important: sase agent list -a is capped at 50 completed entries per project, and status_bucket (not custom status text) determines monitor failures. Historical catalog search is uncapped with -l 0, but it includes stale/old workflow rows and is not proof that an old failed assignment is still unresolved. Reconcile with actual family success, retries, task/bead evidence and local ownership. Do not re-run finished work blindly. This board retains initial failure keys so later failures can be differentiated. Also scan all failures at every check-in so long-running agents that fail after this epoch are not missed by a start-date filter.

Potential ownership hazards from the initial sample: sase-xe.5--plan and --code shared PID 4146228; sase-xe.6--plan and --code shared PID 4115951. Their plan rows can still say RUNNING after a family transition. A stale duplicate row is not a second live worker. Check done.json and current family member before mutation. Waiting epic rows name phase beads; retries must retain bead linkage. Exact-name waits in the toobig chain may need separate reconciliation after a child-hood retry.

Initial recent failures are all monitor rows. They may already have successful follow-ups; this has NOT yet been verified. Zero active monitors at the earlier 23:20Z query; current count is recorded below. Do not replay a failed test monitor without checking the continuation that already handled it.

Message-board PoC friction observed so far: the memory task schema forces path/proposed_change fields despite no memory edit, and ordinary ready status would launch irrelevant memory triage. Append-only attributed notes and one stable bead ID support the baton. Keep the board operational and evaluate this at final completion.

Inventory command: sase agent list -a -j; all projects, no filter. Current raw row counts: {"Running": 12, "Waiting": 29, "Queued": 1, "Done": 44, "Failed": 9}

Counts include supervisor 00a and possible stale family shell rows. Live target scope excludes only the supervision family itself.

